kg:note_ref6531_arrangement_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "In the original arrangement of the Ernst Herzfeld Archive, Travel Journals were included in a larger body of diverse material acknowledged by Ernst Herzfeld as his study collection. In the early 1970s, Joseph Upton, for research purpose, rearranged the collection and created a specific series (Ernst Herzfeld Papers, Series 1: Travel Journals, 1905-1928) for eight travel journals. For some reason, Upton has given this journal an accession number related to the series he created for the notebooks (Ernst Herzfeld Papers, Series 3: Notebooks, 1904-1946, 1957, n.d.), probably following Herzfeld's original organization." .

kg:note_ref6531_odd_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "Ernst Herzfeld Papers, N-84" .

kg:note_ref6531_odd_0_p_1 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "FSA A.6 03.084" .

kg:note_ref6531_odd_0_p_2 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "FSA A.6 01.05" .

kg:note_ref6531_odd_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Additional information from staff reads, \"The handwritten text of the Journal in German was transliterated and reviewed by Ernst Herzfeld's former collaborator, Friedrich Krefter, with the assistance of his wife, Maria Krefter. Please contact the Archives for digital access to the transliterated copy.\"" .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "From Pasargadae to Bushire via Naqsh-i Rustam, Persepolis, Shīrāz, Istakhr, Fīruzābād, Farrāshband, Sar Mashhad, Bīshāpūr, Dā u Dukhtar and Khārg." .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- FSA A.6 01.05, on which Joseph Upton's classification mentions \"N-84\", provides an account of Herzfeld's field work (November 13, 1923 to January 5, 1924) to Shiraz (Iran) and the archaeological sites in its vicinity such as Pasargadae (Iran), Naqsh-i Rustam (Iran), Persepolis (Iran), and Istakhr (Iran), as well as an account of an expedition (March 3, 1924 to April 30, 1924) following the Firuzabad road from Shiraz (Iran) to Fīruzabad (Iran) and Farrashband (Iran), then moving north via Sar Mashhad (Iran), the ancient city of Bishapur (Iran), Nurabad (Iran), and Jinjan (Iran) (also called Jinjun). Finally, departing from Bushihr (Iran) (also called Bushire), Herzfeld visited the Island of Kharg (Iran)." .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_1_p_1 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Original handwritten title on cover reads: \"Ernst Herzfeld; Tagebuch, Persien II, 1923-1924\"" .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_1_p_10 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Daulatabad (Iran): Comparison of ceramics with those at Naqsh-i Rustam, November 25, 1923, (p. 25)." .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_1_p_11 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Firuz Mirza, Nusrat al-daula, son of Ferman Ferma. Visit to Persepolis as Governor-General. of Fars with distinguished company. Also to Naqsh-i Rajab, Naqsh-i Rustam, Istakhr, Band-i Amir via Hadjiabad (Achaemenian ruins). Comments of Prince Firuz on excavations and preservation of monuments, November 26-31, 1923, (pp. 26-31)." .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_1_p_12 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Persepolis (Iran): Preparation of plan; squeezes of all inscriptions-cuneiform, Arabic, Pahlavi and Persian; notes on graffiti, sculptures, ceramics, neighboring ruins. Sketch of plan of terrace (p.47); wing of Ahuramazda symbol with traces of color , November 30 to December 22, 1923, (pp. 31-58)." .

kg:note_ref6531_scopecontent_1_p_13 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "- Shiraz (Iran): Visit to Hāfiziyya (pp.61-62); to Chehel Tan and Haft Tan (p.62 and 72); to Qasr-i Abū Nasr and Barm-i dilak (pp.65-67); references by Persians to ruins in and near Shīrāz (pp.68/69); visit to Mashriqain (pp.69-70), Sketch: pp.69-70; notes on city gates, Khātūn and Shāh Dā'ī allah. Information from British Consul, Mr. Chick, re Sar Mashhad, Khārg, the oil regions, Bushire, Rēshahr, Gumeck, December 23 to March 2, 1923, (pp. 59-72)." .

